# Intentionally empty. This shadows /lib/udev/rules.d/90-alsa-restore.rules,
# which otherwise runs:
#
#   alsactl -E HOME=/run/alsa ... restore <card>
#
# every time a controlC* device is added. alsa-restore.service does the same at
# boot and additionally `alsactl store` at shutdown, which is what writes
# /var/lib/alsa/asound.state - i.e. whatever the codec happened to be doing when
# the box last went down. On this card that state includes `DAC MUTE` = true,
# which silences the wake chime and all TTS.
#
# Ordering lva-audio-init.service After=alsa-restore.service is not enough: the
# card is probed late and the udev restore can land after our unit has already
# applied and verified its settings (on a good boot we won that race by ~20 ms).
# LVA owns this codec's state outright, so there is nothing for alsactl to
# restore: no state file, no restore. setup-module.sh removes the state file and
# masks alsa-restore.service to match.
